<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#vikram-samvat">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vikram Samvat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">57 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Vikrama Samvat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vikram Samvat is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> calendar era that begins in 57 BCE, still widely used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the official calendar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. Tradition links it to a legendary king Vikramaditya of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, but early inscriptions call it the Krita era or the era of the Malava people. It counts lunar months within solar years, and its year numbers run about 57 years ahead of the Common Era.</p></div>
